WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:12.000
Several American towns claim to be the
birthplace of the sundae.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:17.070
Sundaes first appeared in the 1880s, when
the sale of ice cream sodas was banned on

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:22.710
Sundays. Instead, soda fountain operators
poured soda syrup on ice cream, added

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.050
toppings, and called this new treat the
sundae.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:33.140
Today, ice cream sundaes come pre-made and
frozen in individual servings.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.730
There's no need to wait.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.670
This decadent dessert can be served up in an
instant.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.970
At the Sunday Factory.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.390
Production starts with something called the
White Base Mix.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:50.000
It's a pasteurised blend of fresh cream,
milk and a variety of sweeteners.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.460
A worker adds a blend of yellow coloring to
the white base mix, and this takes it from

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.560
bright white to an off white.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:06.230
A generous helping of pure vanilla extract,
lends a distinctive flavor and adds extra

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.850
color to the cream base.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:14.660
After a final stir, the mix is ready for a
freezing process, during which air will be

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.480
added to turn the mix into soft ice cream.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.020
It's time to cue the containers.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:26.630
Two at a time, individual size sundae cups
move forward on the production line.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.170
They meet up with the ice cream dispenser.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.770
It automatically pumps precise amounts of
the soft vanilla ice cream into the cups.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.890
It fills the cups to the three quarters
mark, leaving plenty of room for toppings.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:55.230
It's now time to prepare the next layer of
deliciousness the chocolate fudge sauce.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:01.440
A worker mixes skim milk and sweeteners to a
frothy consistency, and then adds several

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.930
large bags of dark, rich cocoa.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:11.140
The mixer paddle agitates the ingredients to
distribute the cocoa evenly throughout.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.030
The chocolate fudge is pumped into a plastic
tote to cool overnight.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.650
During cooling, the sauce thickens up
substantially.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.380
It's now a rich chocolate fudge syrup.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.600
Velvety smooth.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.740
The chocolate syrup flows out of the tote
and into a big tank.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:42.870
From there, nozzles dispense the chocolate
fudge syrup onto the vanilla ice cream in the

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:50.860
cups. Next, a worker pours a liquid topping
blend into a high speed mixer system that

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.020
beats it to a whipped consistency.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:00.000
Nozzles deposit the whipped topping onto the
syrup covered ice cream.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:06.560
Now it's time to add some chocolate crunch
to the smooth sweet sundae.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.980
The system sprinkles semi-sweet chocolate
chunks onto the whipped topping.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.380
It's the final touch.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.040
These single serving chocolate sundaes have
been prepared almost entirely by machines.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.840
A conveyor now sends them through an icy
blast of air for a quick freeze.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:38.420
Exiting the freezer, the frozen sundaes
travel forward, shielded by overhead guards

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.310
for food safety reasons.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.850
The sundaes funnel into a single file lane.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.820
This positions them to receive lifts.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.580
The lid, slide down a chute and land on top
of the ice cream sundae cups.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.270
Rollers. Press the lids down on the rims.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:05.190
A revolving apparatus deposits plastic
collars around the lids.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:09.590
Moving forward.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.070
The machinery sorts the sundae cups in rows
of two.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:19.280
This allows a robot with suctioning arms to
pick up six at a time and pack them into a

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.600
cardboard box.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:27.580
The boxes travel under and over packing tape
to seal the tops and bottoms.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:38.870
Incredibly, this factory produces 1440 ice
cream sundae cups every 15 minutes.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.780
And it's all thanks to the magic of
mechanization.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:49.860
This company also produces tubs of flavored
ice cream, so there are many mouthwatering

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.380
choices to be made at dessert time.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:59.560
It's a fruit known by many names cactus,
pear, cactus fruit, and prickly pear.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:04.870
It grows on several species of cacti, which
are native to parts of North, central, and

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.130
South America.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.410
Some growers, in addition to selling the
fruit, produce a puree which they sell as a

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.050
flavoring. Prickly pear martini, anyone?

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.180
Cactus pear fruit is high in fiber and rich
in vitamins and minerals.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:26.260
It's sweet and delicious, eaten as is, or as
a natural flavoring in foods and beverages.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:32.710
In the Salinas Valley in California, the
cactus pear harvest begins around late August

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.080
and continues through early April.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.220
The fruit is ripe when its skin begins
turning red.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:44.980
The harvesters wear thick leather gloves to
protect their hands from the thorns, and

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:49.570
safety glasses to shield their eyes from
loose thorns that blow through the air.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:54.460
Tractors haul the cactus pears to the
processor plant.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.220
The fruit first passes over brushes and into
a vacuum that removes loose dirt.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:07.060
Then, through a shower of chlorinated water,
which kills off bacteria, the fruit enters a

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:13.750
cold air dryer for about five seconds, then
passes through a hot air dryer for 20s.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.440
The fruit exits completely dry.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:23.620
A quality control team removes any with
bruises or other cosmetic defects, and

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:26.020
transfers those cactus pears to the puree
line.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:32.950
The fruit that passes inspection falls into
what's called a simulator, a machine that

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.870
lines them up in single file.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:40.450
The simulator deposits each cactus pear into
a cup on a computer guided weigh and sort

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:45.850
machine, which classifies each fruit by
size, then applies the grower's price code

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:52.120
sticker. The fruit then travels on the
conveyor belt that leads to the padded tub

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.530
designated for its weight.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:54.640
Classification in.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:07:01.200
A worker stationed at the tub packs the
cactus pears into a lined shipping box.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.860
Another worker removes any less than perfect
fruit that managed to slip through the

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:10.120
previous checks.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.850
Those also go to the puree line.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.340
On the pure line, the dumper drops the
cactus pears onto a conveyor belt system,

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.680
which transports them to the crusher.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:33.700
The machine crushes the fruit, separating
the skins and flesh.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:39.450
Mashing the flesh into puree and extracting
the sweet magenta colored juice.

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:49.760
From the crusher, the pressed skins drop
onto the vibrating shaker, while the puree

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.670
and juice flow through it into a tank below.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.470
The shaker separates any puree still caught
in the skins.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:06.540
The skins drop into a bin and are hauled off
to be used as compost or sold as animal feed.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:17.610
Once the tank is filled to capacity with
about £400 of pure.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.750
A pump transfers it to a large hopper.

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:26.780
A worker releases puree from the hopper to
the finisher.

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:32.510
The finishers find screens, trap the seeds
while letting the puree pass through to a

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:33.890
holding tank below.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:39.380
The seeds are sold to businesses that
process them into oil for cosmetic and hair

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:43.430
products from the holding tank.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:48.860
The Deseeded puree passes through a second
finisher with even finer filters.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.530
Then it flows into a tank for
pasteurization, which kills off any remaining

00:08:54.530 --> 00:09:01.240
bacteria. The puree is finally ready.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:06.190
A worker fills a drum which is double lined
with plastic bags.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:14.380
She draws for one cup samples from each drum
for quality control tracking.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:27.940
Once a drum contains £400 of puree, the
worker zip ties each bag separately, closes

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:33.010
the drum with a lid, safety seals the lid
with a lock, then puts the drum in the

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:40.790
freezer. The cactus pear puree is sold
frozen to the food and beverage industry,

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:47.360
which uses it to flavor many products, from
ice cream, sorbet and gelato to flavored

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:50.930
water, wine, tequila and brandy.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:58.180
Early versions of pancakes were thin like
crepes.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:05.710
By the 1780s, American cooks were adding a
leavening agent for a fluffier, more filling

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:12.310
pancake. That's when pancakes transitioned
from side dish to main course for the first

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:13.330
meal of the day.

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:19.080
Call them hotcakes, griddle cakes,
flapjacks, or pancakes.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:24.340
There's nothing like a heaping helping of
them splashed with syrup and with frozen

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:28.330
pancakes. Whipping some up for breakfast has
never been easier.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:36.550
Pancake production starts off with the
leavening agent the baking powder.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:42.580
They combine salt, whey, and various
flavorings in a big blender to create a dry

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:44.260
ingredient, pre-mix.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:51.880
Since these will be buttermilk pancakes,
they add powdered buttermilk for flavor.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:59.410
They pump liquid ingredients from storage
tanks into a huge blender.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:10.540
These include water and eggs, sweet cream,
liquified sugar, and soybean oil.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:16.390
They add the dry premix and flour until the
batter reaches the desired consistency.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:22.480
An employee tests the batter's viscosity by
pouring some in a device with a little gate.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:25.910
He opens the gate and measures the flow down
a ramp.

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:30.970
If it runs too thick or too thin, it won't
produce pancakes that are the desired

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:32.890
diameter and uniformity.

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:35.680
The pancake batter passes the viscosity
test.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:41.860
They release the batter from the holding
tank and it flows into a depositor system.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:47.380
The depositor has nine nozzles.

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:52.810
Each nozzle shoots an exact amount of the
pancake batter onto a hot moving griddle.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:12:01.060
Because of the perfect viscosity, each
deposit of batter radiates out to form a neat

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:02.170
round shape.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:07.060
Each one the same size as the next, about
ten centimeters in diameter.

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:15.130
They run three depositors in moving griddles
simultaneously to produce 750 pancakes a

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:19.900
minute. That's an incredible 45,000 pancakes
an hour.

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:29.770
After several seconds on the griddle, one
side is cooked so automated stainless steel

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:32.260
spatulas flip the pancakes over.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.820
There are two sets of flippers.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:41.580
After flipping, the first set repositions
and the second set flips the next row of

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:45.510
pancakes. It's mechanized synchronization.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:51.990
The flippers are spaced apart exactly as the
depositors are.

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:56.910
So the pancakes arrive at the flippers in
the correct order to be picked up and turned

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:05.850
over. Once flipped, the pancakes continue
along the cooking conveyor and the other side

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:12.420
is cooked. It's a short ride for these
pancakes to be completely cooked.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:21.110
From dollops of batter to uniformly rounded
and cooked pancakes.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:24.530
This process has taken just 90s.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:31.310
The pancakes somersault off the cooking
griddle and over a roller to land on the next

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:35.720
conveyor. This somersault exit has a
purpose.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:40.820
It keeps the pancakes from folding over so
they land flat.

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:50.000
Hot off the Griddles the pancakes cool down
on the ride as all three production lines

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.070
merge and head into a freezer.

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:00.230
Inside the freezer.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:05.960
It's a very frigid -28 Celsius and the
pancakes freeze fast.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.350
Now it's over to the packaging line.

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:14.250
This is where the uniformity of these
pancakes pays off.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:19.380
They're similar. Diameter means they can be
neatly packed in groups of six.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:26.040
Machinery seals.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:28.830
Two stacks of six frozen pancakes in one
wrapping.

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:38.850
It has taken less than five minutes to
prepare, freeze and package these pancakes at

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:42.720
home. Prep time in the microwave should be
mere seconds.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.900
Just add syrup and this story has a sweet
ending.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:55.200
Haggis is Scotland's national dish, made from
minced lamb and beef encased in beef

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:01.020
intestine. While quintessentially Scottish
historians believe haggis actually originated

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:05.700
in ancient Greece and eventually made its
way northward with the Romans to Britain.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:13.020
Haggis is traditionally served with mashed
potatoes and turnips, with the Scots call

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:17.370
neeps and tatties, along with a glass of
Scotch whisky to wash it down.

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:20.260
At the haggis factory.

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:23.140
The production process begins with beef fat.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:28.210
They put it through a mechanical chopper,
and a guillotine blade slices it up.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:37.360
The chunks of fat go into a large cook tank.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:41.680
They fill the tank with water and simmer the
fat for 2.5 hours.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:49.970
Then, using a strainer, they drain the
cooked fat and put it in a tub.

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.470
Then they emptied the tub into a mincer.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:01.310
Next they chop up lamb lungs.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:06.440
Haggis recipes sometimes also use heart and
liver.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:14.390
They simmer those in a cook tank as well for
2.5 hours.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:22.800
Using a strainer, they drain and remove the
cooked, chopped lungs and transfer them to a

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:31.390
tub. They now take dried shredded onions and
rehydrate them with some stock from the

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:41.120
cooked meat. They pour the onion and stock
mix into the tub with the cooked lungs, then

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:44.270
empty the whole thing into the mincer to
join the ground fat.

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:58.600
Next to different grinds of oatmeal, medium
and coarse, to give the haggis just the right

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:01.660
texture. Not too stiff, but not too loose.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:07.000
The consistency is key, which is why the
grind proportions are a closely guarded

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:08.140
company secret.

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:12.490
Also a top secret.

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.290
The composition of the spice blend.

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:20.140
They now add. All the company will divulge
about this seasoning is that it includes salt

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:23.200
and pepper. The oatmeal and spice blend.

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:25.570
Now join the other ingredients in the mincer.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:31.480
And there you have it the finished haggis
mix ready to be stuffed into casings.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:38.620
In keeping with tradition, the company
encases its haggis in beef intestines.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:43.890
After a thorough cleaning, the intestines
are stored in salt before use.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:45.820
They soak in cold water overnight to
desalinate.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:52.210
This also softens them up until sufficiently
pliable for the stuffing process.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:56.500
On the filling line, workers slip an
intestine over what's called the stuffing

00:17:56.500 --> 00:18:01.900
horn. Then, with a knee, activate a lever
that triggers the machine to pump out a set

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:03.340
amount of haggis mix.

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:06.640
On this run, they're preparing the £1 size.

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:12.600
They closed the casing on either side with a
thick aluminum staple.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:21.180
Now they spread the haggis out on cooking
trays and pierce each casing to let air

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:23.700
escape as the casing shrinks during cooking.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.610
The temperature inside the steam oven is
230°F.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:39.870
The haggis cooks for one hour, during which
the casing shrinks and forms into a ball

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:46.020
shape. Once the haggis come out of the oven
and cool workers cut them apart.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:54.840
Each haggis goes into a printed plastic bag,
then into a vacuum sealer.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:19:01.550
The machine draws out 99.9% of the air
before sealing the bag.

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:07.850
This vacuum packing process extends a 7 to
10 day shelf life to four weeks.

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:14.110
The packaged haggis now drop into a tank of
hot water.

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:20.650
The bags instantly shrink and thicken by
25%, making them significantly more durable.

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:26.920
Haggis may date back to ancient Greece, but
it certainly changed with the times.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:32.620
This company also makes a one minute
microwavable version, a canapé format, and

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:38.530
even vegetarian haggis far beyond what
Robert Burns could ever have imagined.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:40.370
And.

